            <div xml:id="d329">

                <pb facs="0495" n="181" xml:id="pg181"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/994</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé d’Affaires in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-09-29T15:00:00+08:00">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">September 29, 1914, 3 p.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 7:30 a.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>My telegram of September 28, 5 p.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d329-f1"/> The Foreign
                    Office now advises me that it has received from the Japanese Minister formal
                    notification that, in spite of the understanding in regard to the policing of
                    the Shantung Railway by Chinese troops, the Japanese Government now finds itself
                    compelled by military necessity to take possession of the railway as far west as
                    Tsinan.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
